    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Kushans, a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a>, built an empire from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a> in the 1st–2nd centuries.</li><li>They controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and minted abundant gold coins showing gods of many religions.</li><li>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanishka">Kanishka the Great</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gandhara-art">Gandharan</a> and Mathuran art produced early images of the Buddha, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> spread into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Kushan coins show a remarkable mix of gods, from the Iranian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithra</a> and the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanaya">Nana</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a> and the Buddha, labelled in Bactrian as Boddo.</p></aside>
    </section>

