    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kujula-kadphises"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kujula-kadphises.jpg" alt="Kujula Kadphises" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kujula-kadphises">Kujula Kadphises</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_the_Kushan_king_Kujula_Kadphises.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group;[1], CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kushans were one of five branches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a>, a people driven from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Gansu corridor</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> in the 2nd century BCE, who settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a>, by about 130 BCE. According to the Chinese <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-the-later-han">Hou Hanshu</a></em>, the Yuezhi lands were divided among five <em>xihou</em>, or chiefs, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kujula-kadphises">Qiujiuque</a>, known from his coins as Kujula Kadphises, chief of the Guishuang (Kushan) branch, defeated the other four and made himself king, probably in the first half of the 1st century CE. Kujula then crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> Kush, took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> region and Gandhara from the Indo-Parthian successors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondophares">Gondophares</a> and absorbed the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> and Saka principalities; some of his early coins imitate those of the Indo-Greek king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermaeus">Hermaeus</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabatak-inscription">Rabatak inscription</a>, a Bactrian text discovered in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> in 1993, gives the dynastic line as Kujula Kadphises, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vima-takto">Vima Takto</a>, his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vima-kadphises">Vima Kadphises</a> and great-grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanishka">Kanishka</a>. Under Vima Takto and Vima Kadphises the Kushans advanced into north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a>, and Vima Kadphises introduced the dynasty's gold coinage. Absolute dates remain disputed, but many scholars now place the accession of Kanishka around 127 CE, which fits a rise of Kujula in the decades around 30.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mathura"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mathura.jpg" alt="Mathura" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vishram_Ghat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Umang108, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kushan Empire joined three economic worlds: the oasis cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, the irrigated plains of Gandhara and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> valley around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a>. Its rulers controlled routes that linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han China</a>, through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>, with the Parthian lands and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, as well as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> ports from which ships sailed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>; the 1st-century <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea">Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</a></em> describes the trade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbarikon">Barbarikon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharuch">Barygaza</a> in goods from the interior. A hoard found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapisa">Begram</a>, ancient Kapisa, containing Roman glass and bronzes, Indian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivories</a> and Chinese lacquer, illustrates the range of this exchange, though its date and nature are debated. Kushan gold coins, minted in large quantities from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vima-kadphises">Vima Kadphises</a> onward on a standard close to that of the Roman aureus, circulated widely. Urban life flourished at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>, Mathura, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/termez">Termez</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Bactra</a>, and irrigation works expanded farming in Bactria. Religious life was plural: the coins show Iranian deities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithra</a> (Miiro), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharro">Pharro</a> and the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanaya">Nana</a>, the Indian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oesho">Oesho</a>, usually identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, Greek gods such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helios">Helios</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>, and the Buddha, labelled <em>Boddo</em>. Buddhist monasteries, Brahmanical and Jain communities at Mathura and Iranian fire cults all received patronage.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kanishka"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kanishka.jpg" alt="Kanishka" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kanishka">Kanishka</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KanishkaCoin3.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">No machine-readable author provided. World Imaging assumed (based on copyright claims)., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kushan power rested on the mounted warfare of their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> ancestors, combining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-archer">horse archers</a> and armoured cavalry with infantry and elephants raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and on garrisoned cities and fortified sites along the routes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> and Gandhara. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kujula-kadphises">Kujula Kadphises</a> built the empire by conquest, defeating the Indo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> and taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, Gandhara and probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a>, while his successors pushed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> and further down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabatak-inscription">Rabatak inscription</a> claims that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanishka">Kanishka</a>'s authority reached cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saketa">Saketa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kausambi">Kausambi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>, though how far this meant direct rule rather than recognition is uncertain. The Kushans also looked north-east to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>. According to the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-the-later-han">Hou Hanshu</a></em>, a Kushan king who had been refused a Han princess sent his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> Xie with 70,000 men across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamir-mountains">Pamirs</a> in 90 CE against the Han general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ban-chao">Ban Chao</a>, who cut off their supplies and forced them to withdraw, after which the Kushans sent tribute to the Han court. Later Buddhist legend credits Kanishka with victories over the Parthians and in the Tarim, but these stories are not reliable history. In the 3rd century Kushan armies proved unable to stop the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Persians</a>, who conquered Bactria and the lands west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> by the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a> (c. 240–270).</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rabatak-inscription"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rabatak-inscription.jpg" alt="Rabatak inscription" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rabatak-inscription">Rabatak inscription</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Surkh_Kotal_inscription.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, 2nd century CE inscription., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kushan kings combined titles from every tradition they ruled: the Indian <em>maharaja</em>, 'great king', the Iranian '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negus-nagast">king of kings</a>', <em>devaputra</em>, 'son of the gods', possibly modelled on the Chinese 'son of heaven', and on one inscription of a later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanishka">Kanishka</a> even <em>kaisara</em>, 'Caesar'. Royal power was made sacred through a dynastic cult: at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surkh-kotal">Surkh Kotal</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> and at Mat near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> the Kushans built sanctuaries (<em>devakula</em>) with statues of the kings, including the headless statue of Kanishka now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-museum-mathura">Mathura Museum</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabatak-inscription">Rabatak inscription</a> records Kanishka's order to build such a shrine with images of gods and of his ancestors. Kanishka also replaced Greek with Bactrian, written in Greek script, as the language of royal coinage and inscriptions, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gandhari-language">Gandhari Prakrit</a> in Kharosthi script and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> in Brahmi were used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Provinces were governed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a> (<em>kshatrapa</em>) and great satraps (<em>mahakshatrapa</em>), such as Kharapallana and Vanaspara, named on a Buddhist statue dedicated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarnath">Sarnath</a> in Kanishka's third year. Inscriptions dated in Kanishka's era across Bactria and India show an administration able to impose a common reckoning of time. The main royal centres were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Purushapura</a> (Peshawar), Mathura and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapisa">Kapisa</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Bactra</a> and Surkh Kotal in the north.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kanishka-stupa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kanishka-stupa.jpg" alt="Kanishka stupa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kanishka-stupa">Kanishka stupa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ShahJiKiDheriStupa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kushan period was decisive for the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and of Asian art. Under Kushan rule the workshops of Gandhara, drawing on Hellenistic models, and those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a>, rooted in Indian traditions, produced some of the earliest images of the Buddha in human form, a development that shaped Buddhist sculpture from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Buddhist tradition remembers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanishka">Kanishka</a> as a great patron: his stupa at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanishka-stupa">Shah-ji-ki-Dheri</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a> was among the wonders of the Buddhist world, admired by Chinese pilgrims, and its bronze relic casket, inscribed with Kanishka's name, was excavated in 1908–1909. Tradition also links Kanishka with a Buddhist council in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and with the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashvaghosha">Ashvaghosha</a>, though both associations are uncertain. Monks from the Kushan lands carried Buddhist texts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> ideas along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lokaksema">Lokaksema</a>, a Kushan monk active in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> in the 170s and 180s, made some of the earliest Chinese translations of Mahayana sutras. Kushan gold coinage served as a model for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushano-sasanian-kingdom">Kushano-Sasanians</a>, the Kidarites and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a>, whose early gold coins adapt Kushan designs, and Kushan titles and prestige were claimed by later rulers of the region. Bactrian, raised to an imperial language by Kanishka, remained in written use in the region until about the 9th century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vasudeva-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vasudeva-i.jpg" alt="Vasudeva I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vasudeva-i">Vasudeva I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VASUDEVA_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Xarbold, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The empire's decline began after the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasudeva-i">Vasudeva I</a>, the last great Kushan king, whose inscriptions date roughly from 190 to 230; his name, unlike those of his predecessors, is purely Indian, reflecting the dynasty's growing Indianisation. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-three-kingdoms">Sanguozhi</a></em> records that an envoy of Bodiao, king of the Da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a>, usually identified with Vasudeva, reached the Wei court in 229, the last significant Kushan contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. In the 3rd century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Persians</a>, after overthrowing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> in 224, advanced east: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a>'s inscription at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-rostam">Naqsh-e Rostam</a> claims Kushanshahr as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushano-sasanian-kingdom">Kushano-Sasanian Kingdom</a>, governed by Sasanian princes who styled themselves Kushanshah. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> Kushan authority contracted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, Gandhara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> valley passed to local powers such as the Nagas and later to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a> pillar inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samudragupta">Samudragupta</a> mentions the homage of a 'Daivaputra-Shahi-Shahanushahi', generally taken to be a late Kushan ruler. The Kushan kingdoms that survived under rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasishka">Vasishka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasudeva-ii">Vasudeva II</a> were finally overrun in the later 4th century by the Kidarites, a new Hunnic or Chionite power that claimed the Kushan heritage, and the empire's history closed around 375.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kushan Empire united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, Gandhara and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> under a Yuezhi dynasty, controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> crossroads between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and patronised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gandhara-art">Gandharan</a> and Mathuran art in which the Buddha was first widely shown in human form, helping carry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</em></p>

